To build a custom React hook that handles debounce functionality on an API call to fetch data from an external source (e.g., an API endpoint), you can use the `useEffect` hook along with the `useState` hook to manage state changes.

Here is how your component might look:

```jsx
import { useState } from "react";

function CustomDebounceHook() {
    const [data, setData] = useState(null);

    // Debounce function to delay fetching data until after a certain interval
    const debouncedDataFetch = useRef();

    useEffect(() => {
        if (!debouncedDataFetch.current) return;

        async function fetchData() {
            await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 500)); // Delaying the request up to 1 second

            try {
                const response = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');
                const json = await response.json();

                setData(json);
            }
            catch(error) {
                console.error("Error fetching data:", error);
            }

            debouncedDataFetch.current = null; // Clear reference to avoid infinite loops
        }

        fetchData(); // Fetch data immediately when mounted
    }, []);

    return (
        <div>
            {/* Your UI components */}
        </div>
    );
}

export default CustomDebounceHook;
```

In this example:
- The `useState` hook manages the state (`data`) which will hold the fetched JSON object.
- The `useRef` hook creates a ref named `debouncedDataFetch`, which we'll use later inside our useEffect hook to clear it out once the data has been successfully retrieved.
- Inside the useEffect hook, we first check whether the `ref` exists before calling the `fetchData()` function. If not, we create one using `setTimeout`. This ensures that the function doesn't run indefinitely even if there was no previous data available.
- We then make a network request to fetch the data, wait for its completion, and update the state accordingly. After updating the state, we remove the reference to prevent any further calls to the same URL.
- Finally, we render whatever UI elements you want based on the current value of the `data` state variable.

This setup allows for smooth handling of asynchronous requests while ensuring that the component only updates when necessary due to the debounce effect provided by the `useEffect` hook.

